<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guadeloupe Fund</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1815–1983</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Guadeloupefonden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Guadeloupe Fund was a sum of 24 million francs that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1815 as compensation for giving up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> island of Guadeloupe, granted to it in 1813 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> assigned the money to Crown Prince Charles John and his heirs, and from 1815 the Swedish state paid the Bernadotte family an annual sum until it was finally redeemed in 1983.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guadeloupe-fund.jpg" alt="Guadeloupe Fund" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fredric Westin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_XIV_Johan,_king_of_Sweden_and_Norway,_painted_by_Fredric_Westin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
